CONTENT={
'motion_direction':dict(title='From local correspondence to cardinal motion decisions',refs=['salzman'],
 intro='A direction judgement requires relating structure across observations. An isolated random-dot image carries no unique displacement vector. This task therefore provides a direct test of whether a shared recurrent visual system transforms temporal correspondence into a categorical motion decision. Its four alternatives distinguish direction coding from a simple detector of visual change.',
 rationale='The primary psychometric variable is dot displacement in input pixels. Small translations challenge spatial correspondence; large translations within the correspondence bank should produce a concentrated population over candidate offsets. We ask where the direction becomes linearly readable: the appearance encoder, the fused visual tokens, spatial recurrent memory, or the global recurrent state. A strong separation between appearance and fused representations would localize the useful computation to the temporal pathway.',
 interpretation='The movie has two observations. The terminal decision therefore supplies only one post-evidence sample. Confidence thresholds characterize certainty at that sample; they cannot yield a richly resolved latency distribution. The mechanistic comparison is the increase in direction information between the first and second observations. Opposite-quadrant priority changes test how much distributed dot evidence is required for a correct judgement.',
 next='A useful follow-up would vary coherence and dot lifetime independently of displacement. That would distinguish tolerance of weak correspondence from tolerance of directional noise. Direction-selective perturbations of the motion population would additionally test feature-specific causal influence, complementing the spatial interventions reported here.'),
'orientation':dict(title='Signed orientation comparison through recurrent visual memory',refs=['frequency'],
 intro='The observer must determine the sign of an orientation change across two gratings. The answer is relational: the absolute orientation of either grating alone is insufficient. The same positive and negative rotations must be interpreted across different starting angles and phases, creating a compact test of visual comparison.',
 rationale='We vary rotation magnitude and measure correct sign reports. Orientation is axial: angles separated by 180 degrees describe the same grating. Consequently, a continuous neural target should use cosine and sine of twice the angle. The model need not explicitly represent an angle to solve the task; successful readout from the fused or recurrent representations can instead arise from distributed image features.',
 interpretation='Spatial attention in a full-field grating determines which image regions contribute to the comparison. The fixed quadrant manipulations test that spatial selection. Memory perturbations between the two observations test whether a preserved first-frame description is necessary. A fall in accuracy after a recurrent-state reset, together with strong final decoding, would support a sequential comparison computation.',
 next='Future tests should rotate the entire coordinate frame, alter spatial frequency, and independently change contrast. Those transfers would assess whether the learned sign judgement follows orientation itself across nuisance transformations.'),
'contrast':dict(title='Contrast comparison, spatial selection and recurrent state dependence',refs=['contrast','normalization'],
 intro='Contrast describes variation around an image brightness baseline. The task asks which of two grating intervals has the stronger contrast. Because the absolute pedestal varies, success requires comparing the two observations rather than applying one fixed intensity criterion.',
 rationale='The contrast increment is the primary difficulty variable. Separating sensitivity from interval preference is essential: a tendency to choose the second interval can change overall accuracy when samples are imbalanced. We therefore retain confusion matrices, class-balanced accuracy, and probability-based discrimination. Spatial priority interventions test whether the model can perform the comparison using a restricted part of the grating.',
 interpretation='The appearance pathway and local contrast statistics both provide signals relevant to this task. Their fusion is then routed through the shared attention map. The population analyses ask whether the answer is already readable in visual features or becomes more explicit after recurrent integration. A global additive priority shift is an exact control because softmax removes any common offset.',
 next='A pedestal-by-increment factorial would resolve whether thresholds follow a Weber-like relationship. Contrast polarity reversals and independently varied mean luminance would further separate contrast comparison from brightness cues.'),
'spatial_frequency':dict(title='Spatial-frequency discrimination in a shared visual architecture',refs=['frequency','contrast'],
 intro='Spatial frequency measures how rapidly a pattern changes across space. In this task the observer chooses the interval containing the finer grating. The required representation must remain useful across starting frequency, orientation and phase, while retaining the relative difference between intervals.',
 rationale='Difficulty is expressed as an octave difference, a logarithmic ratio of frequencies. Equal octave steps correspond to equal multiplicative changes. The psychometric function tests whether frequency discrimination degrades smoothly as the two patterns converge. Population decoding identifies where the interval ordering becomes accessible.',
 interpretation='A response to local edges can be sensitive to both orientation and frequency. The task-specific head must select the relevant relation from their shared representation. Memory-state perturbations identify which recurrent component carries the comparison forward. Spatial quadrant manipulations assess how the representation tolerates restricting the evidence available for its decision.',
 next='Testing novel pedestal frequencies and contrast levels would distinguish a general comparison rule from interpolation over familiar gratings. Aliasing should be measured explicitly when approaching the raster resolution limit.'),
'chromatic_increment':dict(title='Chromatic interval comparison under shared spatial priority',refs=['color'],
 intro='The observer identifies the interval with a positive increment along a defined color axis. The stimulus construction holds the intended luminance relation fixed while changing RGB components. The task therefore tests whether a representation shared with achromatic and motion tasks preserves useful chromatic information.',
 rationale='The psychometric variable is the magnitude of the linear-RGB increment. A color increment is a vector in the renderer color space, so its numerical magnitude depends on that definition. We measure discrimination across the renderer values and inspect whether the appearance and fused pathways support an independent linear readout of the answer.',
 interpretation='The model receives RGB images while the correspondence bank operates on grayscale averages. Chromatic information can enter through learned appearance features and local RGB statistics. The causal analyses ask whether spatial priority still regulates performance when the defining signal lies in those static channels. This is a direct architectural test of shared selection across visual features.',
 next='Additional axes and calibrated display-space transformations would test transfer beyond the trained color direction. Joint chromatic and motion conflicts would be especially informative about competition within the common attention map.'),
'contour':dict(title='Contour integration across local elements and recurrent populations',refs=['field','treisman'],
 intro='A contour is a spatial relationship among elements. The observer chooses which interval contains the aligned Gabor path among surrounding elements. Local orientation signals must be integrated into evidence for a coherent configuration, making this task a test of organization beyond a single isolated feature.',
 rationale='The perturbation axis is alignment jitter. Here increasing the numerical value makes the contour less orderly and typically makes detection harder. We preserve that direction in the plots. The analysis compares information in visual tokens with information in the spatial recurrent field and global state, then tests the causal cost of redistributing priority across the display.',
 interpretation='The spatial recurrent network can combine neighboring patch representations through its convolutional gates. The CLS state receives attention-weighted summaries of those representations. A population code that separates contour from distractor intervals need not consist of a single contour-selective unit; the decoding and state-space analyses are designed to expose distributed organization.',
 next='Changing contour length, spacing, curvature and local orientation jitter separately would test the spatial extent of integration. Occluding a contiguous path segment versus equally many scattered elements would isolate configuration sensitivity.'),
'natural_spectrum':dict(title='Natural-image spectral comparison and the retention of texture statistics',refs=['spectrum'],
 intro='Natural images contain structure at many spatial scales. This task asks which interval has greater relative high-frequency detail after a controlled change to its spectral slope. The judgement concerns a statistical property of the image rather than object identity.',
 rationale='The slope difference sets difficulty. We retain the original natural-image split and evaluate new procedural comparisons. Readout from appearance, fused features and memory reveals whether the model carries a sufficient description of spectral content across intervals. Decoder training and testing use separate source-image splits.',
 interpretation='A spatial priority map may emphasize textured regions that contain strong evidence about image detail. Averaging pixels across unrelated photographs would obscure that evidence, so the atlas pairs a single native image with its own attention and places the condition-average attention beside it. Those panels answer complementary questions about one trial and about systematic spatial preference.',
 next='Testing matched amplitude spectra with altered phase structure would separate sensitivity to spectral amplitude from sensitivity to recognizable content. Spatially localized filtering could reveal whether the selected region controls the judgement.'),
'orientation_ring':dict(title='Location-cued orientation comparison with a persistent ring cue',refs=['spatial','normalization'],
 intro='The observer reports the sign of rotation at a ring-cued Gabor location. Distractor locations also change, so a successful comparison must preserve which location is relevant. The task links a spatial instruction to a feature-specific report.',
 rationale='Rotation magnitude controls sensory difficulty. We decode the cued location independently of the response label, since remembering where to inspect and determining how the target rotated are distinct computations. The attention atlas follows the cue, sample and comparison in their native order.',
 interpretation='The shared recurrent map can carry location-specific evidence while the CLS state summarizes selected information. Spatial priority manipulations at the target and a designated distractor test competition between those locations. Resetting memory immediately before the probe assesses whether pre-probe processing contributes to the comparison.',
 next='Removing the ring before the sample and adding longer retention intervals would separate cue visibility from remembered priority. Independent target and distractor rotations would support a full distractor-congruence analysis.'),
'orientation_cued':dict(title='Rule-dependent orientation judgement across a retention interval',refs=['spatial','stokes'],
 intro='This task combines a location instruction with a requested rotation sign. A positive report requires that the target rotate in the instructed direction. Target preservation, opposite rotation and distracting changes create competing sources of evidence, while blank intervals require the instruction and sample to persist.',
 rationale='We examine rotation magnitude, delay duration, cue-location decoding, remembered orientation and the final instructed response. The key mechanistic distinction is between preserving the visual sample and preserving the rule that determines how to interpret it. Time-generalization matrices ask whether readable information maintains a common population code across the delay.',
 interpretation='Attention manipulations are separated by encoding, delay and evidence epochs. Encoding interventions can alter what enters memory; delay interventions can change ongoing selection and memory writes; probe interventions can alter which comparison influences the decision. A reciprocal target cost and distractor benefit would support competitive spatial selection.',
 next='A full factorial of cue sign, target rotation and foil congruence would distinguish mnemonic degradation from rule confusion. Applying a late countercue would test how rapidly the stored representation can be read under a different instruction.'),
'motion_duration_cued':dict(title='Temporal voting over cued motion and maintenance of the winning direction',refs=['salzman','spatial'],
 intro='The cued motion patch changes cardinal direction across eight transitions. The observer must report the direction occupying the greatest number of transitions. A single transition is insufficient; the task requires accumulating evidence over time and preserving the winning direction through a later blank interval.',
 rationale='Native conditions vary retention delay. The temporal decoder shows when the eventual winner becomes readable, while the confidence analysis asks when the unchanged terminal readout first commits to an alternative. Because later transitions can overturn an early lead, premature confidence is an informative property of the computation.',
 interpretation='Spatial selection determines which patch contributes evidence, whereas temporal integration determines how successive directions affect the state. The comparison-memory branch and recurrent states can make different contributions. Reset and gain perturbations at the retention boundary test the stored result after the visible evidence has ended.',
 next='Varying the winner margin, order of transitions and total sequence length independently would reveal whether the model counts evidence, averages motion populations, or disproportionately weights recent observations.'),
'krauzlis_cued_motion':dict(title='Target selection and distractor rejection in cued motion-change detection',refs=['arcizet','sc'],
 intro='A precue identifies one of two moving-dot patches. The observer reports a change in that patch and withholds a change report for foil-only changes and catches. This makes selection behaviorally essential: strong evidence that a change occurred somewhere is not sufficient to answer correctly.',
 rationale='We vary motion-change magnitude and preserve target, foil and catch events as separate categories. Hits measure detection of relevant changes; reports on foil trials measure distractor capture; catch reports measure spontaneous false alarms. Their separation permits a precise test of the proposed dual effect of spatial perturbation.',
 interpretation='The long baseline makes delayed local motion comparisons available. One shared spatial distribution gates both learned visual values and explicit comparison features. Inhibiting a selected site can reduce its contribution while increasing the relative contribution of the other site. The causal test is whether these competing changes in contribution produce reciprocal behavioral costs and benefits.',
 next='A decisive follow-up would compare matched short and long baselines while perturbing the comparison branch independently of attention. That design would separate temporal evidence availability from the allocation of shared spatial priority.'),
'spatial_binding':dict(title='Retrospective selection of an orientation-location binding',refs=['treisman','spatial'],
 intro='The observer sees an array of orientations, retains it through a blank interval, then receives a retrospective cue and a probe. The report concerns whether the orientation at the queried location was exchanged with another location. The orientation inventory is preserved, so recognizing the set of features alone cannot solve the task.',
 rationale='The critical memory is a conjunction of what and where. All locations are initially eligible because the query arrives after retention. We examine delay-dependent accuracy and the availability of location-specific sample information in spatial memory and CLS state. The retrospective cue offers a natural landmark for changes in population geometry.',
 interpretation='A distributed spatial memory can maintain several local descriptions before the query selects one. Delay lesions therefore probe storage, while probe-period priority changes probe selection of the stored binding. If target localization becomes decodable only after the cue, that is appropriate to the trial information structure rather than evidence of delayed perception.',
 next='Set size and swap distance should be varied independently. The present native generator draws a rotated inventory of four orientations spaced 45 degrees apart and permutes their locations. A follow-up with independently sampled orientations would test memory precision across a richer array distribution.'),
'image_recognition':dict(title='Recognition of an exact natural-image sample among intervening images',refs=['wm','it'],
 intro='The observer studies a sequence of natural images and then judges whether the repeated probe raster appeared in that sequence. Study load changes the number of images that must remain available. Probe duration changes the time allowed to evaluate the retrieval signal.',
 rationale='Load and probe hold are analyzed separately. The zero-load condition is an always-novel control; recognition sensitivity requires both seen and novel trials. Neural analyses distinguish readable information about the last study image from readable evidence that the later probe belongs to the study set. These are different targets for a memory decoder.',
 interpretation='A trajectory that changes with each study image may still support recognition if old information is retained in a distributed population. The 8-by-8 content reconstruction is a coarse image descriptor, whereas the behavioral label requires exact membership in the original raster sequence. Attention maps are therefore shown on individual photographs, with separate location-averaged panels.',
 next='Matched lure similarity and controlled intervening-image interference would extend the measured serial-position and false-alarm profiles. Reconstructing every study item would test how multiple stored images contribute to the recognition decision beyond the last-item probe reported here.'),
'vda4':dict(title='Value-cued priority and causal competition in a four-location change task',refs=['rvit','sc','normalization','wolfe'],
 intro='Four Gabor locations compete for processing under a cue that carries probability and value information. The report concerns a change anywhere in the array. A cue can therefore improve the allocation of resources while also making an unexpected change elsewhere easier to miss.',
 rationale='Native angle conditions quantify performance under the trained cue distribution. The central counterfactual fixes a 100 percent bottom-right cue and places a change at top left. Inhibiting bottom-right priority during the two change frames tests whether releasing resources from the expected location improves unexpected-change reports.',
 interpretation='The counterfactual includes cued-change, uncued-change and catch trials on matched nuisance seeds. A benefit for uncued changes accompanied by a cost for cued changes is a redistribution signature. Catch reports show whether the intervention also shifts the response criterion. These seven-frame movies keep the long-window comparison branch at zero, locating any effect in the recurrent visual-attention computation.',
 next='Variable trial length would allow the same cue manipulation to recruit the delayed comparison branch. Independently changing cue validity and reward value would separate probability-guided allocation from reward-guided allocation.'),
'vda9':dict(title='Priority competition and unexpected changes across nine visual locations',refs=['rvit','sc','normalization','wolfe'],
 intro='The nine-location task expands the number of candidate change sites while preserving a probabilistic and value-bearing cue. The shared priority distribution must allocate a fixed total mass across a larger display, creating a stronger test of spatial competition than a four-site array.',
 rationale='We measure native discrimination over angle conditions and probe the same corner-to-corner counterfactual used in the four-location task. The exact mapping from native image cells to model patches is retained. Since nine locations do not divide the ten-by-ten model grid evenly, affected patch counts are part of the intervention geometry.',
 interpretation='Spatial allocation should be read relative to the area occupied by each stimulus cell. An equal additive bias per patch is a precise computational dose, while total priority mass can differ across locations with different patch counts. Cue-only, sample, delay and change-epoch manipulations distinguish instruction processing from selection of sensory evidence.',
 next='Matched-area interventions and trial-length manipulations would determine how much of a difference from VDA4 is explained by geometry, stimulus competition, or available temporal computation.'),
'vda16':dict(title='Shared spatial priority under sixteen-location change competition',refs=['rvit','sc','normalization','wolfe'],
 intro='Sixteen Gabor locations place the greatest spatial competition demand on the value-directed sub-battery. Each stimulus occupies a smaller region of the common visual representation. The model must compare local evidence while respecting the probabilistic cue and reporting changes anywhere.',
 rationale='The analyses preserve native cell geometry and distinguish changes at the cued and uncued locations. Set size is fixed within this task, so comparisons to VDA4 and VDA9 form a cross-task load comparison. They also change the spatial sampling of each Gabor, which is measured through the patch allocation maps.',
 interpretation='A strong cue can concentrate the attention distribution on a small part of a crowded display. Inhibition can release that concentration, but successful detection elsewhere still requires sufficient sensory information at the new location. Thus a redistribution of attention and a behavioral benefit are separate empirical outcomes.',
 next='Rendering the same physical Gabor size while changing set size would isolate competition from resolution. Coupling that manipulation to longer sample and comparison periods would test whether more recurrent processing compensates for crowding.'),
'memory_guided_search':dict(title='Object memory, feature conjunctions and the selection of a matching location',refs=['it','treisman','wolfe'],
 intro='The observer first sees an object, retains its appearance, and then reports its location among four candidates. The arrays contain feature combinations, irregular objects or procedurally generated textures. Distinct and similar candidates vary the specificity required of the remembered template.',
 rationale='The full factorial crosses stimulus family, similarity, sample duration, blank delay and array exposure. This is essential for localizing the failure: poor brief-sample performance suggests encoding limits; a selective delay cost suggests retention limits; improvement with longer array exposure suggests retrieval or comparison limits. Family-specific neural analyses retain these distinctions.',
 interpretation='A target-location decoder applied before the array tests a variable the agent cannot yet determine from the visible sample alone. Sample-content reconstruction is therefore the more direct pre-array memory measure. During search, cue-content information must be translated into a spatial selection. Comparing spatial memory, CLS decoding and target attention can expose a separation between storing a template and using it to locate a match.',
 next='The matched-array query experiment establishes how the sample affects selection with search pixels fixed. Candidate-matching decoders and targeted memory-to-query interventions can now test which retained features mediate that effect. Additional object transformations would assess tolerance beyond the exact-raster matching rule.'),
'popout_search':dict(title='Feature contrast, exposure time and location reports in pop-out search',refs=['treisman','wolfe'],
 intro='One item differs from the other three in color or orientation. The observer reports its location. There is no preceding sample to remember, so the task isolates selection from simultaneous feature contrast in an array.',
 rationale='We cross feature difference with array exposure. A benefit from exposure duration can arise from more stable selection or more complete readout even when the target is physically present from the first array frame. We therefore examine both time-resolved attention and the evolving location code.',
 interpretation='The task uses four locations in every trial. Its behavioral curves establish sensitivity and exposure dependence at that set size. The attention interventions reveal whether directing priority toward or away from the target changes localization, while decoding tests whether the target is represented before it dominates the selected state.',
 next='A true search-efficiency experiment should vary set size, distractor heterogeneity and target prevalence. That would allow slopes of decision time against item count to be compared across feature conditions.'),
'motion_memory_search':dict(title='Remembered motion as a template for selection among moving alternatives',refs=['salzman','it','spatial'],
 intro='The sample is a moving-dot patch. After a blank delay, four independent moving-dot patches appear, and the observer must locate the one with the remembered direction. The dot layouts differ between sample and array, so an exact raster match cannot provide the solution.',
 rationale='Direction spacing controls the similarity of alternatives. Sample duration, retention delay and search duration are independently varied. The remembered continuous direction is decoded as cosine and sine of its angle, alongside final location and coarse sample-image content. Those targets distinguish motion memory from memory for a particular dot layout.',
 interpretation='The motion pathway must create a representation that generalizes across dot realizations, and the recurrent system must maintain it until new candidates appear. A difference between direction decodability and localization accuracy would identify a retrieval or routing bottleneck. Attention manipulations at the correct array location and an alternative quantify causal competition during matching.',
 next='Novel speeds, coherence levels and direction spacings would test the invariances of the motion template. Extending the identical-array query experiment to those transformations would test whether memory-guided selection follows direction across changed motion statistics.')
}

NEUROSCIENCE={
'motion_direction':'Salzman and colleagues showed that stimulating direction-selective MT populations can bias monkeys toward the preferred motion direction of those populations. This motivates linking a representation to behavior by intervention as well as by decoding. In the present architecture, temporal correspondence is first represented locally and then selected spatially. The two operations predict different perturbation signatures: a change in selected location changes which evidence is pooled, whereas a direction-selective change would alter the content of that evidence. Our spatial experiment tests the first causal step. The appearance-to-fusion decoding comparison identifies where the temporal relationship first becomes accessible to a simple readout.',
'orientation':'Neurons selective for oriented structure provide a useful conceptual starting point for an orientation-comparison task. The computational requirement extends beyond detecting an edge: the observer must preserve a description of one pattern and compare it with another. A neuroscientific analysis therefore asks whether response selectivity emerges during the comparison or is already present during the reference. Memory reset separates these alternatives experimentally. If a correct answer survives the removal of one recurrent component, another route may carry the reference or sufficient comparison information. Simultaneous recordings across the architecture make those routes directly inspectable.',
'contrast':'Campbell and Robson connected grating visibility to spatial-frequency content, while normalization models explain how stimulus drive and attentional allocation interact. This suggests analyzing both the physical contrast increment and the location from which evidence is selected. A priority manipulation could influence a contrast judgement by favoring reliable portions of the grating, by altering memory writes, or by changing the selected readout. Its behavioral effect should therefore be compared with the state at the same phase. The present recordings allow a distinction between a clear contrast-related representation and successful use of that representation by the decision pathway.',
'spatial_frequency':'De Valois and colleagues measured spatial-frequency selectivity in macaque visual cortex. Such selectivity provides a population basis for distinguishing coarse and fine patterns. For a two-interval judgement, however, the useful variable is their relation. The strongest mechanistic prediction is a progression from pattern-dependent activity to an interval-ordering code. Our regional decoders test that progression on held-out images, while memory perturbations test dependence on the earlier observation. A high terminal AUC in the global state can coexist with broad tuning in earlier visual features because the later state can combine many weak feature signals.',
'chromatic_increment':'Lennie and colleagues characterized chromatic mechanisms in macaque striate cortex. The relevant lesson for this experiment is that color can be represented across a population of responses with different sensitivities. The analysis therefore follows chromatic discrimination through a shared architecture rather than seeking a single dedicated color unit. Spatial priority acts on a site containing multiple kinds of visual evidence. A causal influence of that priority on a chromatic judgement would demonstrate a common selection operation even though the defining sensory signal differs from the moving-dot tasks.',
'contour':'Field, Hayes and Hess investigated contour integration using relationships between local oriented elements. This motivates treating the arrangement of features as an experimentally controlled variable. The model spatial recurrent field has an explicit neighborhood structure, which makes it a candidate substrate for integrating these relationships. The global state could then summarize the organized evidence. By measuring both population information and sensitivity to memory reset, the analysis can distinguish a contour representation that is present locally from one that has already been consolidated into a decision-relevant global signal.',
'natural_spectrum':'Field related natural-image statistics to cortical response properties. Here that connection motivates a controlled spectral manipulation rather than an unrestricted image-category judgement. The same image can differ in the distribution of energy across scales while retaining recognizable structure. A useful population code should distinguish the relevant statistical change across different scenes. Keeping separate source-image splits for probe fitting and testing asks whether a linear readout generalizes across those scenes. Attention overlays then reveal which regions of a particular photograph contribute to the selected evidence.',
'orientation_ring':'Funahashi and colleagues described mnemonic spatial coding in monkey prefrontal cortex. A location instruction can therefore be analyzed as information that persists beyond its first presentation and constrains subsequent processing. In this task, cue and rotation variables can be decoded separately. Their temporal ordering matters: a location code during the sample establishes access to the instruction, whereas an answer code after the probe reflects the instructed comparison. The intervention epochs exploit this distinction to test whether priority is most consequential during encoding or during final selection.',
'orientation_cued':'Studies of dynamic prefrontal coding motivate tracking representations across the entire sequence rather than inspecting only a terminal state. The location and sign instruction can remain functionally available even while the pattern of recurrent activity changes. A decoder trained during one phase may therefore transfer well, poorly, or only after the relevant cue reappears. The time-generalization matrix tests this geometry explicitly. Resetting a state during the blank interval adds a causal test of maintenance: a subsequent performance loss identifies dependence on that state at a time when the task-relevant sample is absent.',
'motion_duration_cued':'The task combines a sensory motion judgement with a memory-guided spatial instruction. A single direction-selective response is insufficient to identify the most frequent transition unless the evidence is integrated across the movie. This motivates examining the trajectory of the eventual winning class and the point at which that winner becomes decodable. During a blank delay, a stable answer code would be compatible with maintaining a summarized decision; a changing code with successful cross-temporal decoding would support maintenance in a dynamically evolving population. Mid-delay state perturbation tests whether that maintained variable is behaviorally necessary.',
'krauzlis_cued_motion':'Arcizet and Krauzlis studied covert spatial selection in a cued motion-change paradigm, and reversible inactivation studies establish causal roles for the superior colliculus and frontal eye fields. These experiments motivate a distinction between detecting a physical change and selecting the behaviorally relevant change. A model can contain clear foil evidence and still correctly withhold a positive report. The most diagnostic intervention therefore measures hits, foil-driven errors and catches separately. Inhibiting the target and inhibiting the foil need not have symmetric behavioral consequences, because the instruction assigns different meanings to the same physical event at the two sites.',
'spatial_binding':'Feature-integration and spatial-memory work motivate representing an object together with its location. A swap changes the assignment of features to positions while preserving the inventory of features. The retrospective query makes this a useful test of distributed storage: before the cue, all locations must remain available. A model that stores only an average orientation or a histogram of orientations would lose the binding needed for the response. The spatial-memory recordings and mid-delay perturbations address whether a location-preserving state supports the later query.',
'image_recognition':'Miller and colleagues studied visual working memory using delayed matching in macaque prefrontal cortex. That tradition motivates separating sample-related activity, retention, and a match-dependent response to the probe. In a multi-image study sequence, each new image can displace, transform or coexist with earlier information. Population trajectories track that evolution, and load curves quantify its behavioral consequences. The recognition decision concerns membership of the entire study set, while the continuous decoder asks the narrower question of whether a coarse description of the last study image remains recoverable. Reporting both prevents those two memory questions from being conflated.',
'vda4':'Superior-colliculus attention experiments motivate perturbing a spatially organized priority signal while measuring perception. The important computational prediction is competitive: lowering priority at one location can change the influence of evidence elsewhere. The four-location layout makes the corner-to-corner test especially clear. A cue establishes an expectation, a controlled event violates or satisfies it, and the intervention acts only when new evidence appears. Reciprocal effects on cued and uncued changes would support redistribution. The catch condition distinguishes such redistribution from a general increase in positive reports.',
'vda9':'The larger array asks whether the same spatial-priority computation scales with the number of competing sites. Neuroscientific causal experiments often compare effects inside and outside the affected field; our corner-to-corner design makes that comparison explicit. The grid also imposes an important geometric constraint: the nine stimulus cells occupy different numbers of the model ten-by-ten sites. Measuring probability mass and documenting the masks therefore makes the intervention reproducible. The behavioral analysis then determines whether a geometrically specified redistribution benefits an unexpected location.',
'vda16':'A dense array creates two potentially interacting demands: several locations compete for priority, and each stimulus is represented by fewer spatial samples. The attention intervention directly changes competition while preserving the rendered movie. A benefit at an unexpected location would show that useful evidence was present but underweighted. An attention shift without a behavioral benefit would instead motivate closer examination of the quality of the newly selected representation. This distinction links causal manipulation to population decoding rather than treating a visible movement of the map as sufficient evidence of improved processing.',
'memory_guided_search':'Chelazzi and colleagues connected remembered target information with visual selection in inferior temporal cortex; feature-integration and Guided Search work motivate considering both templates and competition among candidates. In this model, a remembered object must influence a spatial choice only after an array appears. Encoding an object and using that encoding as a search template are therefore separate achievements. The factorial timing design, content reconstruction, target-location decoding and causal interventions are organized around this separation. A preserved memory with poor target selection would motivate changing how memory participates in the query that selects visual evidence.',
'popout_search':'Feature-integration and Guided Search theories distinguish feature-based guidance from the later identification or response process. This task provides a controlled case with one differing feature among three matching distractors. Exposure duration can affect either the emergence of a useful priority distribution or the downstream conversion of that distribution into a location report. Recording both attention and recurrent answer information allows these possibilities to be compared. Because the target is present from the first array frame, a delayed location code can be interpreted against an unchanging physical stimulus.',
'motion_memory_search':'Motion physiology supplies a basis for representing direction, while memory-guided search motivates using a remembered feature to select among alternatives. Their combination is demanding because the sample and candidates have independent dot arrangements. The useful memory must describe motion in a form that survives this change in image realization. A circular direction decoder directly tests that information, while a location decoder tests the later use of the template. The delay and exposure manipulations then distinguish retention of direction from the process of finding the matching moving patch.'
}
